### How do you build a queue from two stacks?

Keep an input stack and an output stack. Enqueue pushes to input. Dequeue checks output; if empty, pour input into output (reversing order), then pop output.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class QueueViaStacks<T> {
    private final Deque<T> in = new ArrayDeque<>();
    private final Deque<T> out = new ArrayDeque<>();

    public void enqueue(T item) { in.push(item); }

    public T dequeue() {
        if (out.isEmpty()) while (!in.isEmpty()) out.push(in.pop());
        return out.pop();
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() { return in.isEmpty() && out.isEmpty(); }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        QueueViaStacks<String> q = new QueueViaStacks<>();
        q.enqueue("A"); q.enqueue("B"); q.enqueue("C");
        System.out.println(q.dequeue()); // A
        System.out.println(q.dequeue()); // B
        q.enqueue("D");
        System.out.println(q.dequeue()); // C
        System.out.println(q.dequeue()); // D
    }
}
```

```text
A
B
C
D
```

### How do you build a stack from one queue?

On push, add the element to the queue, then rotate by polling and re-offering all earlier elements. The newest element ends up at the front, so poll gives LIFO order.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Queue;

public class StackViaQueue<T> {
    private final Queue<T> q = new ArrayDeque<>();

    public void push(T item) {
        int size = q.size();
        q.offer(item);
        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) q.offer(q.poll());
    }

    public T pop() { return q.poll(); }
    public T top() { return q.peek(); }
    public boolean isEmpty() { return q.isEmpty(); }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        StackViaQueue<String> s = new StackViaQueue<>();
        s.push("Bottom"); s.push("Middle"); s.push("Top");
        System.out.println(s.pop()); // Top (LIFO)
        System.out.println(s.pop()); // Middle
    }
}
```

```text
Top
Middle
```

### What is the amortized analysis for the two-stack queue?

Each element: 1 push to input (O(1)), 1 transfer to output (O(1) amortized), 1 pop from output (O(1)). Total per element = O(1). One dequeue may trigger an O(n) transfer, but it happens at most once per element.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class AmortizedExplanation {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Per element: push(1) + transfer(1) + pop(1) = O(1)");
        System.out.println("One dequeue may cost O(n), but rare and amortized out");
    }
}
```

```text
Per element: push(1) + transfer(1) + pop(1) = O(1)
One dequeue may cost O(n), but rare and amortized out
```

### What are the complexity comparisons?

The two-stack queue has amortized O(1) for both operations. The queue-based stack has O(n) push and O(1) pop. There is no way to get both operations constant for the queue-to-stack direction.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class ComplexityTable {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Queue from 2 stacks:");
        System.out.println("  enqueue: O(1), dequeue: amortized O(1)");
        System.out.println("Stack from 1 queue:");
        System.out.println("  push: O(n), pop: O(1)");
    }
}
```

```text
Queue from 2 stacks:
  enqueue: O(1), dequeue: amortized O(1)
Stack from 1 queue:
  push: O(n), pop: O(1)
```

### Why is lazy transfer critical?

Transferring only when the output stack is empty ensures elements move once. Eager transfer mixes newly enqueued items with older ones already in the output stack, breaking FIFO order.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class LazyTransferRule {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Lazy: transfer only when output empty");
        System.out.println("  Each element moves exactly once -> amortized O(1)");
        System.out.println("Eager: transfer on every dequeue");
        System.out.println("  New items mixed with old -> FIFO broken, O(n) every time");
    }
}
```

```text
Lazy: transfer only when output empty
  Each element moves exactly once -> amortized O(1)
Eager: transfer on every dequeue
  New items mixed with old -> FIFO broken, O(n) every time
```

### What are common mistakes?

Eager transfer is the primary bug. Checking only one stack in `isEmpty()` is another. Using these constructions in production Java when `ArrayDeque` is available is a third.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class InterconvertMistakes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Mistake 1: eager transfer -> breaks order");
        System.out.println("Mistake 2: isEmpty checks only one stack");
        System.out.println("Mistake 3: using this in production instead of ArrayDeque");
    }
}
```

```text
Mistake 1: eager transfer -> breaks order
Mistake 2: isEmpty checks only one stack
Mistake 3: using this in production instead of ArrayDeque
```

### How is this applied in banking?

At the National Bank of India, a constrained teller component exposed only stack primitives. Customer service requests needed oldest-first fairness. The two-stack queue gave FIFO semantics with amortized O(1) serving, so a single occasional bulk transfer never hurt throughput.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class TellerDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        QueueViaStacks<String> teller = new QueueViaStacks<>();
        teller.enqueue("Rajesh: balance inquiry");
        teller.enqueue("Priya: loan application");
        teller.enqueue("Pankaj: card replacement");

        System.out.println("Serving: " + teller.dequeue());
        System.out.println("Serving: " + teller.dequeue());
        System.out.println("Serving: " + teller.dequeue());
    }
}
```

```text
Serving: Rajesh: balance inquiry
Serving: Priya: loan application
Serving: Pankaj: card replacement
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

The two-stack queue is the Banker's queue in functional data structures, where two lists serve as the two stacks. The key interview insight is amortized analysis: worst-case O(n) per call does not mean O(n) average. Stacks and queues are inter-convertible, proving neither is strictly more powerful.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class SeniorInterconvert {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Functional: Banker's queue = two lists");
        System.out.println("Key: amortized O(1) despite worst-case O(n)");
        System.out.println("Result: stacks and queues are inter-convertible");
        System.out.println("Production: just use ArrayDeque");
    }
}
```

```text
Functional: Banker's queue = two lists
Key: amortized O(1) despite worst-case O(n)
Result: stacks and queues are inter-convertible
Production: just use ArrayDeque
```
